2. TYPESCRIPT
--------------------------------------------------------

>> Type vs Interface
- Dono object shapes define karte hain.
- Interface: extend ho sakta hai, declaration merging support karta hai (same naam se dobara declare karo to merge ho jayega).
- Type: union, intersection, tuples, primitives define kar sakta hai jo interface nahi kar sakta.
- Rule of thumb: public API/object shape ke liye interface, complex types (union/intersection) ke liye type.

>> Any vs Unknown vs Never
- any: type checking completely off kar deta hai - risky, avoid karna chahiye.
- unknown: type-safe alternative to any - use karne se pehle type narrow karna padta hai (typeof/instanceof check).
- never: value jo kabhi occur hi nahi hogi - function jo throw karta hai ya infinite loop, ya exhaustive switch case ka default.

>> Union & Intersection
- Union (|): value in se kisi ek type ka ho sakta hai. Example: string | number.
- Intersection (&): saare types ke properties combine hote hain ek object me. Example: TypeA & TypeB.
- Union discriminated types banane me useful hai (tagged unions).

>> Generics
- Reusable, type-safe components banane ke liye jo multiple types ke saath kaam karein.
- Example: function identity<T>(arg: T): T { return arg; }
- Generic constraints: <T extends SomeType> se type restrict kar sakte hain.
- Utility types (Array<T>, Promise<T>) generics ka hi use karte hain.

>> Enums
- Named constants ka set define karta hai.
- Numeric enum (default 0,1,2...) aur String enum dono possible.
- const enum - compile time pe inline ho jata hai, extra JS code generate nahi hota.
- Alternative: "as const" union types, jo modern TS me zyada preferred hai (tree-shakeable).

>> Type Guards
- Runtime check jo TypeScript ko batata hai ki variable kis specific type ka hai.
- typeof guard - primitives ke liye.
- instanceof guard - class instances ke liye.
- in operator - object me property check karne ke liye.
- Custom type guard: function isX(val): val is X { ... } - "is" keyword use karke.

>> Utility Types
- Partial<T> - saari properties optional bana deta hai.
- Required<T> - saari properties mandatory bana deta hai.
- Pick<T, K> - specific properties select karta hai.
- Omit<T, K> - specific properties exclude karta hai.
- Readonly<T> - saari properties readonly bana deta hai.
- Record<K, V> - key-value map type banata hai.

>> Optional/Readonly Properties
- Optional (?): property present ho ya na ho - interface Foo { name?: string }.
- Readonly: property ek baar assign hone ke baad change nahi ho sakti - readonly id: string.
- Function parameters bhi optional ho sakte hain (param?: type).

>> Classes, Abstract Classes, Interfaces
- Class: blueprint hai object banane ka, properties + methods hote hain, constructor hota hai.
- Access modifiers: public (default), private (sirf class ke andar), protected (class + subclasses).
- Abstract class: instantiate nahi ho sakti directly, base class provide karti hai jisse other classes extend karti hain, abstract methods implement karna mandatory hota hai child class me.
- Interface: sirf shape/contract define karta hai, implementation nahi deta - class "implements" interface.
- Class ek interface implement kar sakti hai, sirf ek class extend kar sakti hai (multiple interfaces implement ho sakti hain).

