3. AWS LAMBDA
--------------------------------------------------------

>> Lambda Basics
- Serverless compute service - server manage kiye bina code run karna, event trigger hone par.
- Pay-per-use billing (request count + execution duration/memory).
- Stateless - har invocation independent hoti hai (unless warm container reuse ho raha ho).

>> Handler
- Entry point function jo Lambda invoke karti hai: exports.handler = async (event, context) => {...}
- "event" - trigger data (API Gateway request, S3 event, etc.)
- "context" - runtime info (requestId, remaining time, memory limit, etc.)

>> Runtime
- Managed runtimes available: Node.js, Python, Java, Go, .NET, Ruby, custom runtime (bootstrap).
- Node.js runtime versions upgrade karte rehna important hai (deprecated runtimes retire ho jate hain).

>> Layers
- Shared code/dependencies (node_modules, libraries) ko separate zip me package karke multiple functions me reuse karna.
- Max 5 layers per function, unzipped size limit 250MB (layers + function code combined).
- Common use: shared utility functions, heavy SDK dependencies.

>> Environment Variables
- Key-value pairs jo runtime pe function ko available hote hain (process.env.VAR_NAME).
- Config values (DB endpoints, table names) ko hardcode karne ke bajaye env vars me rakhna best practice.
- Sensitive data ke liye encrypted env vars (KMS) ya Secrets Manager/Parameter Store use karna better hai.

>> Cold Start
- Jab Lambda function pehli baar invoke hoti hai (ya baad me scale hone par new instance), naya execution environment initialize hota hai - isme extra latency lagti hai.
- Factors: runtime, package size, VPC config (VPC cold starts zyada slow the, ab improved hain with Hyperplane ENI), language (Node.js/Python fast, Java/dotnet slower).
- Mitigation: Provisioned Concurrency, smaller deployment package, avoid unnecessary VPC, keep dependencies minimal, SnapStart (Java).

>> Timeout & Memory
- Max timeout 15 minutes.
- Memory 128MB se 10240MB (10GB) tak configurable - CPU power memory ke proportional allocate hoti hai.
- Zyada memory = zyada CPU = kabhi kabhi faster execution + kam cost even though per-ms rate zyada hai.

>> Concurrency
- Concurrency = ek time pe kitne function instances simultaneously execute ho rahe hain.
- Account-level default limit hota hai (soft limit, increase request kar sakte ho AWS se).
- Unreserved concurrency saari functions share karti hain by default.

>> Reserved/Provisioned Concurrency
- Reserved Concurrency: specific function ke liye concurrency limit fix/guarantee karna (dusre functions ka concurrency pool isse reduce hota hai). Overuse se throttle hoga.
- Provisioned Concurrency: pre-warmed instances ready rakhta hai taaki cold start na ho - predictable low-latency traffic ke liye use hota hai (extra cost lagta hai).

>> IAM Permissions
- Har Lambda function ka ek Execution Role hota hai jo define karta hai wo kaunse AWS resources access kar sakti hai.
- Principle of least privilege follow karna chahiye - sirf jitni permission chahiye utni hi dena.
- Resource-based policy bhi hoti hai jo define karti hai kaun Lambda ko invoke kar sakta hai (e.g., S3, API Gateway ko permission).

>> Lambda Retries
- Async invocations (S3, SNS, EventBridge): automatic retry hota hai (default 2 retries) with delays.
- Sync invocations (API Gateway): retry client responsibility hoti hai, Lambda khud retry nahi karta.
- Stream-based (Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams): success tak retry hota rehta hai (blocking) unless bypassBatchOnFunctionError configure ho.

>> Dead-Letter Queues (DLQ)
- Async invocation fail hone ke baad (saare retries exhaust hone ke baad) event ko SQS queue ya SNS topic me bhej dete hain for later inspection/reprocessing.
- Newer alternative: "Lambda Destinations" jo success/failure dono cases handle kar sakta hai aur zyada flexible hai (SQS, SNS, Lambda, EventBridge target ho sakte hain).

>> Lambda + S3
- S3 event notification (ObjectCreated, ObjectRemoved etc.) Lambda ko trigger kar sakti hai.
- Common pattern: file upload -> S3 trigger -> Lambda processes file (resize image, parse CSV, etc.)
- Lambda ko S3 bucket access ke liye IAM role me s3:GetObject/PutObject permission chahiye.

>> Lambda + API Gateway
- API Gateway HTTP requests ko Lambda function tak route karta hai (Lambda Proxy Integration common pattern hai).
- Proxy integration me poora request (headers, body, query params) Lambda ko "event" object ke through milta hai, aur Lambda ko specific format me response return karna padta hai (statusCode, headers, body).
- Non-proxy integration me request/response mapping templates define karne padte hain (zyada control but complex).

