4. EVENT-DRIVEN ARCHITECTURE
--------------------------------------------------------

>> Events
- Ek event = system me kuch hua iska record/notification (e.g., "OrderPlaced", "UserSignedUp").
- Immutable fact hoti hai - past me kya hua uska data.

>> Producers & Consumers
- Producer: event generate/publish karta hai (e.g., order service).
- Consumer: event ko subscribe karke react karta hai (e.g., inventory service, notification service).
- Producer ko consumer ke baare me pata nahi hota (decoupling).

>> Event Bus
- Central hub jahan events publish hote hain aur interested consumers ko route/deliver hote hain.
- AWS me EventBridge ya SNS is role me use hota hai.

>> EventBridge
- Fully managed serverless event bus service.
- Multiple sources se events le sakta hai: AWS services, custom apps, SaaS partners.
- Event pattern matching ke through rules define karke specific targets ko route karta hai.

>> EventBridge Rules
- Rule = condition + target(s). Jab bhi koi event pattern match kare, rule us event ko defined target(s) ko forward karta hai.
- Schedule-based rules bhi bana sakte hain (cron/rate expression) - jaise scheduled Lambda triggers.

>> Event Patterns
- JSON structure jo define karta hai kaunse events match karenge (source, detail-type, detail fields pe filter).
- Example: source: "aws.s3", detail-type: "Object Created" jaise pattern se specific events filter hote hain.

>> Targets
- Rule match hone ke baad event kahan jayega - Lambda, SQS, SNS, Step Functions, Kinesis, another event bus, API destination, etc.
- Ek rule multiple targets tak event bhej sakta hai (fan-out pattern).

>> Synchronous vs Asynchronous
- Synchronous: caller wait karta hai response ke liye (e.g., API Gateway -> Lambda direct call).
- Asynchronous: caller event fire karke aage badh jata hai, response ka wait nahi karta (e.g., EventBridge, SNS, SQS based flows).
- Event-driven architecture mostly async communication use karti hai for loose coupling & scalability.

>> Loose Coupling
- Services ek dusre ko directly nahi jaante - sirf events ke through communicate karte hain.
- Benefit: services independently develop/deploy/scale ho sakti hain, ek service fail hone se poora system down nahi hota.

>> Idempotency
- Same operation multiple baar perform karne par bhi result same rahe (side-effects duplicate na ho).
- Important hai kyunki events duplicate deliver ho sakte hain (at-least-once delivery guarantee common hai).
- Implementation: idempotency key check karna (e.g., DynamoDB me processed event IDs track karna) before processing.

>> Retry & Failure Handling
- Consumer failure pe event retry hota hai (based on service - SQS visibility timeout, Lambda async retries, etc.)
- Exponential backoff strategy common hai retries ke beech.
- Poison messages (jo baar baar fail hote hain) ko handle karne ke liye DLQ zaroori hai.

>> Dead-Letter Queues
- Failed events (jo saare retries ke baad bhi process nahi ho paye) ek separate queue me store hote hain for manual inspection/reprocessing.
- SQS, SNS, Lambda, EventBridge sabme DLQ configure kar sakte hain.

