6. DYNAMODB
--------------------------------------------------------

>> Table / Item / Attribute
- Table = collection of items (NoSQL, schema-less except keys).
- Item = ek row jaisa (JSON-like document, unique primary key ke saath).
- Attribute = item ke andar ek field/column.

>> Partition Key
- Primary key ka hash component - data distribute karne ke liye internally use hota hai partitions ke across.
- Same partition key = same partition me data store hota hai. Good distribution zaroori hai "hot partition" avoid karne ke liye.

>> Sort Key
- Partition key ke saath combine hoke composite primary key banata hai.
- Same partition key ke multiple items ko sort key se order/range query kar sakte hain (begins_with, between, etc.)

>> Query vs Scan
- Query: specific partition key (+ optional sort key condition) ke basis pe efficient lookup - fast & cost-effective.
- Scan: poori table read karta hai phir filter apply karta hai - slow & expensive, large tables pe avoid karna chahiye.

>> GSI (Global Secondary Index)
- Different partition key/sort key combination define karta hai jo base table se alag ho, cross-partition queries allow karta hai.
- Apna throughput capacity hota hai, eventually consistent reads only.

>> LSI (Local Secondary Index)
- Same partition key but different sort key base table ke.
- Table creation time pe hi define karna padta hai (baad me add nahi kar sakte).
- Strongly consistent reads support karta hai (GSI nahi karta).

>> TTL (Time To Live)
- Attribute define karke items ko automatically expire/delete karwa sakte hain specific timestamp ke baad.
- Background process hai, thoda delay ho sakta hai actual deletion me - cost-free cleanup mechanism.

>> Conditional Writes
- Write operation tabhi succeed ho jab specific condition true ho (e.g., attribute_not_exists, value match).
- Race conditions avoid karne ke liye use hota hai (optimistic locking pattern).

>> Consistent vs Eventually Consistent Reads
- Eventually consistent (default): fast, cheap, but abhi-abhi hue write ka latest data turant nahi mil sakta.
- Strongly consistent: latest write guarantee milta hai but zyada latency/cost, aur GSI pe available nahi hai.

>> Single-Table Design
- DynamoDB best practice - multiple entity types (users, orders, products) ko ek hi table me store karna, overloaded partition/sort keys use karke.
- Joins avoid karne ke liye aur access patterns ke basis pe pre-design karte hain (NoSQL me joins costly/impossible hote hain).

>> Access-Pattern Design
- DynamoDB design "access pattern first" approach follow karta hai - pehle decide karo queries kya honge, phir table/index design karo (SQL ke ulta, jaha normalize karke baad me query sochte hain).

