7. AWS S3
--------------------------------------------------------

>> Buckets & Objects
- Bucket = top-level container (globally unique name).
- Object = actual file/data stored with a key (path-like identifier) + metadata.

>> Presigned URLs
- Temporary URL jo limited time ke liye specific S3 object ko access (GET/PUT) karne ki permission deta hai bina AWS credentials share kiye.
- Common use case: client directly S3 pe file upload kare (Lambda ke through proxy kiye bina) - bandwidth/cost efficient.

>> Versioning
- Enable karne par same key ke multiple versions store hote hain (overwrite/delete pe bhi purana version retrievable rehta hai).
- Accidental deletion se protect karta hai (delete marker create hota hai, actual data rehta hai).

>> Lifecycle
- Rules define karke objects automatically transition (Standard -> IA -> Glacier) ya expire/delete kar sakte hain time ke basis pe - cost optimization ke liye.

>> Encryption
- At rest: SSE-S3 (S3-managed keys), SSE-KMS (customer-managed via KMS), SSE-C (customer-provided keys).
- In transit: HTTPS/TLS.

>> S3 -> Lambda Trigger
- Object create/delete events par Lambda automatically invoke ho sakta hai (event notification configuration).
- Async invocation hoti hai - retries applicable (as discussed above).

>> S3 Permissions/IAM
- Bucket policy (resource-based, bucket level access control) vs IAM policy (identity-based, user/role level).
- ACLs bhi hote hain but modern practice me avoid kiya jata hai (bucket policy/IAM preferred).
- Block Public Access settings by default enabled hone chahiye security ke liye.

